---
layout: changelog
title: PostgreSQL 18.7 and 17.12 security update rolled out to every managed database
date: 2026-11-15
tags: security, databases
---

On November 12, the PostgreSQL project released 18.7 and 17.12, which fix [52 security vulnerabilities](https://www.postgresql.org/support/security/). Most are rated High (CVSS 8.8), and many let a database user who can sign in run arbitrary code on the server. One more lets a client that hasn't signed in make a PostgreSQL 18 server restart.

**Appwrite Cloud** has rolled the patched build out to every managed [PostgreSQL](/docs/products/databases/postgresql) database, in every region. The update is mandatory, so there is nothing to opt into:

- PostgreSQL 18 databases move to 18.7, and PostgreSQL 17 databases move to 17.12.
- It is a patch release within the same major version. Your data, extensions, connection strings, and credentials stay the same.
- A database switches at its next restart, such as waking from idle, a failover, or a resize. The update adds no downtime of its own.

To switch right away, [pause and resume](/docs/products/databases/postgresql/maintenance#pause-resume) the database, which stops it briefly. Run `SELECT version();` to see which version it is on.

The PostgreSQL release also fixes index corruption from earlier versions. After your database is on the new version, run `REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY` on any of these you use:

- GiST indexes that use `gist_trgm_ops` from `pg_trgm`
- B-tree indexes on `bpchar` columns declared without a length (`char(n)` is not affected)
- GIN indexes that have returned missing or unrelated rows

